[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Resilience Patterns: Circuit Breakers, Rate Limiting, and Backpressure"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern distributed systems are prone to cascading failures. When a downstream service slows down or fails completely, dependent services can exhaust their resource pools, leading to widespread outages. Implementing resilience patterns is essential for robust problem-solving in microservices architectures."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Circuit Breakers"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Circuit Breaker pattern prevents an application from repeatedly trying to execute an operation that is likely to fail. It operates in three states: Closed (normal operation), Open (fast-failing requests), and Half-Open (testing recovery)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class CircuitBreaker {
    constructor(threshold, timeout) {
        this.threshold = threshold;
        this.timeout = timeout;
        this.state = "CLOSED";
        this.failureCount = 0;
        this.nextAttempt = Date.now();
    }

    async execute(action) {
        if (this.state === "OPEN") {
            if (Date.now() > this.nextAttempt) {
                this.state = "HALF-OPEN";
            } else {
                throw new Error("Circuit breaker is OPEN");
            }
        }

        try {
            const result = await action();
            this.reset();
            return result;
        } catch (error) {
            this.recordFailure();
            throw error;
        }
    }

    reset() {
        this.state = "CLOSED";
        this.failureCount = 0;
    }

    recordFailure() {
        this.failureCount++;
        if (this.failureCount >= this.threshold) {
            this.state = "OPEN";
            this.nextAttempt = Date.now() + this.timeout;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Rate Limiting"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Rate limiting restricts the number of requests a client can make to an API within a specified time window. This protects services from denial-of-service conditions, brute-force attacks, and unexpected traffic surges. Common algorithms include Token Bucket and Leaky Bucket."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Token Bucket: Allows bursts of traffic up to the bucket capacity while maintaining a steady average rate."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fixed Window: Resets the request counter at fixed intervals (e.g., every minute)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sliding Window Log: Tracks a rolling time window for higher precision without edge-case spikes."},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"3. Backpressure"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When data producers generate data faster than consumers can process it, backpressure mechanisms allow the consumer to signal the producer to slow down. Without backpressure, systems risk running out of memory (OutOfMemoryError)."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const { Readable, Writable } = require('stream');

const readableStream = new Readable({
    read(size) {
        // Produce data
        this.push('data chunk\n');
    }
});

const writableStream = new Writable({
    write(chunk, encoding, callback) {
        // Simulate slow processing
        setTimeout(() => {
            console.log('Processed:', chunk.toString().trim());
            callback();
        }, 1000);
    }
});

// Piping connects streams and automatically handles backpressure
readableStream.pipe(writableStream);"},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Combining circuit breakers, rate limiting, and backpressure creates a defensive architecture capable of absorbing failures gracefully. When designing distributed applications, apply these patterns at boundaries where services interact with external dependencies or unreliable networks."}]
